WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.981
For every mega-expensive blockbuster grossing
more than a billion dollars worldwide, there's

00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:10.300
always a mega-expensive box office flop greeted
by moviegoers with all the enthusiasm of Tax

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.300
Day.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.760
Now, every major studio film seems to cost
upwards of 100 million dollars to produce,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.489
and inevitably, many movies never come close
to making that money back.

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:23.330
These are the films whose production bills
and astronomical marketing budgets landed

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.589
them on the list of the biggest flops of the
past 15 years.

00:00:27.589 --> 00:00:28.589
Speed Racer

00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:33.930
2008's Speed Racer was a risky proposition
to begin with: an adaptation of a manga and

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.440
anime from the late '60s, with little known
Emile Hirsch in the lead.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.629
Studio Village Roadshow and distributor Warner
Brothers, however, had no qualms about throwing

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:42.680
money at the vehicle.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:48.420
Along with a reported $120 million price tag,
Speed Racer additionally had one of the most

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.600
expensive advertising campaigns and media
blitzes ever for a film.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.820
The gambit failed spectacularly, as Speed
Racer was among the first in a long line of

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.570
casualties who dared to duel Marvel Studios.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:04.460
Go, go, go Speed Racer didn't, with a mere
$18 million opening weekend.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.500
Making matters worse, it was demolished by
Iron Man in its second week of release.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.860
The Chronicles of Narnia: Prince Caspian opening
the following weekend didn't help matters

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.040
either, and Speed Racer stalled out to a $94
million worldwide gross.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.380
The flop ultimately lost Village Roadshow
and Warner Brothers a cool $100 million.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.380
Sahara

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.440
It's the kind of pulpy fun that should have
made for an entertaining flick.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.330
Sahara was an attempt to kick-start a new
adventure franchise in the mold of Indiana

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.960
Jones, but trouble started brewing long before
the film hit theaters in 2005.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.110
The film's initial budget of $80 million swelled
to $130 million, and author Clive Cussler

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.880
— who hadn't been given the final say over
the script he'd been promised — tacked on

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.330
millions in legal fees suing the producers
during an unsuccessful bid to block the film's

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:50.330
release.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.070
"Finally I said, 'This is going down the tube.'

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.180
The scripts are getting worse and worse and
worse.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.100
Finally I just pulled myself away."

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.140
Even the ever-affable Matthew McConaughey
wasn't enough to attract audiences.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.890
Adding insult to injury, the legal snafu and
associated fees wormed its way through various

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.550
courts for eight more years until ultimately
being thrown out in 2013.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:17.920
With a worldwide gross of only $119 million,
Sahara was no oasis, amassing a loss just

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.790
north of $100 million.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.100
How Do You Know

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.490
There's no denying television and film renaissance
man James L. Brooks is comedy royalty.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.420
For TV he helped create The Mary Tyler Moore
Show and Taxi, and on the big screen he delivered

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.660
thoughtful comedies like Broadcast News.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.640
But if you're unfamiliar with his 2010 effort
How Do You Know, you're not alone.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.410
Despite a cast featuring Paul Rudd, Reese
Witherspoon, Owen Wilson, and even Jack Nicholson,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:46.840
the romantic comedy barely sparked, collecting
just under $49 million worldwide.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.850
For most rom-coms that tally isn't terrible.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:55.460
But How Do You Know cost an insane $120 million
to produce, due in part to the whopping salaries

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.980
commanded by its stars.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.340
Brooks also took the unusual step of reshooting
the beginning and end of the film, pushing

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.959
the budget into the stratosphere.

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:07.819
Audiences found the romantic comedy neither
romantic nor funny as the movie ultimately

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:10.280
lost $105 million for Sony.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.420
"That's fantastic."

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.350
"Yep that's a good one."

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.690
"That's great."

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:17.690
Ben-Hur

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.130
The 1959 epic Ben-Hur is an unassailable legend,
and at its time, was the most expensive film

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.130
ever made.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.209
Creating an update to the Charlton Heston
epic after 57 years, however, proved to be

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.490
a major miscalculation for co-financing studios
Paramount and MGM.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:37.900
The production delivered plenty of CGI spectacle,
but it was hampered by absolutely toxic reviews.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:42.470
The remake was taken out in its opening weekend
by Suicide Squad, and the debut of smash hit

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.560
Don't Breathe the following week sealed its
tragic fate.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:51.120
Ben Hur's eventual $94 million gross wasn't
even enough to cover its $100 million budget,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.709
and the movie eventually took an estimated
$120 million loss.

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:56.709
The Alamo

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:01.160
Based on the historic San Antonio siege, Disney
had big plans for The Alamo.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.570
The studio lined up Ron Howard as director,
tapped red-hot Russell Crowe to star, and

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.080
strategically set a 2003 holiday season release
date.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.970
Exactly none of those things worked out.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:16.489
Then-Disney chief Michael Eisner balked at
the $125 million budget, and slashed costs

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:21.049
by hiring the much-more-affordable John Lee
Hancock to direct, and ditched Crowe in favor

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:22.729
of Billy Bob Thornton.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:26.990
Unfortunately, Eisner's caution was no match
for the public and critical indifference toward

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.830
the film, with one critic calling it,

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:33.270
"The equivalent of one long drone from a stiff
Social Studies teacher who can scarcely be

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.789
bothered to add any sort of relevance to the
topic."

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:37.590
Who remembers the Alamo?

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:43.419
Apparently moviegoers didn't as the movie
only grossed a paltry $25 million worldwide.

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:48.330
Analysts speculate Disney flushed $120 million
down the drain on the picture, making it one

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.789
of the biggest bombs ever at the time.

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:53.039
Sinbad: Legend of the Seven Seas

00:04:53.039 --> 00:04:58.069
DreamWorks Animation once looked like a champion
of hand-drawn animation in an era when it

00:04:58.069 --> 00:04:59.669
had been all but completely phased out.

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:03.759
The Prince of Egypt was a hand-drawn hit,
and The Road to El Dorado looked to keep the

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:04.759
streak going.

00:05:04.759 --> 00:05:09.610
But their success came to an abrupt end with
2003's Sinbad: Legend of the Seven Seas.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:14.580
With a modest $60 million budget and a heavyweight
voice cast, DreamWorks felt confident pouring

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.080
ridiculous amounts of money into a massive
worldwide marketing campaign and promotional

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.080
tie-ins.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.979
The film opened against stiff competition
with the likes of Terminator 3 and Legally

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:24.979
Blonde 2.

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:28.789
Sinbad fell overboard, earning only $80 million
worldwide.

00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:33.849
Because of its huge marketing budget, the
movie washed out with a $125 million loss

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:37.199
and DreamWorks cut ties with oldschool animation.

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:38.909
Mars Needs Moms

00:05:38.909 --> 00:05:42.219
Mars Needs Moms had "giant gamble" written
all over it.

00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:46.319
Despite being produced by the legendary Bob
Zemeckis, the story of a young boy who must

00:05:46.319 --> 00:05:50.069
rescue his mother from Martians failed to
strike a chord with audiences.

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:54.789
Negative reviews cited the animation, which
many moviegoers found flat and mechanical.

00:05:54.789 --> 00:06:00.009
With a whopping $150 million budget, the film
would have had to overperform just to turn

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:01.009
a profit.

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:05.569
But Disney executives found they needed antacid
instead of moms after the film pulled in just

00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:07.570
$39 million worldwide.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:12.319
Putting a serious dent in the House of Mouse's
2011 earnings, Mars Needs Moms never left

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:16.460
the launch pad and finished $130 million in
the hole.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.460
Tomorrowland

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.909
In 2015, Disney returned to the world of massive
flops with Tomorrowland, a film with a pedigree

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:26.629
so solid the studio could be forgiven for
its misplaced confidence.

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:30.839
Co-written and directed by The Incredibles
Oscar-winner Brad Bird, and featuring a cast

00:06:30.839 --> 00:06:33.240
including George Clooney and Hugh Laurie.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.439
Tomorrowland promised an exciting, brain-bending
ride through alternate dimensions — and

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:39.860
somehow managed to fall disappointingly short.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.499
"This part's gonna get a little weird."

00:06:42.499 --> 00:06:46.319
The film had the dubious honor of opening
at number one during the worst Memorial Day

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:48.889
weekend for Hollywood in recent memory.

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:52.479
Critics panned the film for being maudlin,
preachy and not adventurous enough to live

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:53.569
up to its premise.

00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:59.750
Tomorrowland made $209 million worldwide,
but that barely covered its $190 million budget,

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:05.059
and analysts estimate Disney saw $140 million
vaporize on the movie.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.060
The Lone Ranger

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.660
"Hi ho silver, Away!"

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.699
In any discussion of the most ill-conceived
would-be blockbusters of all time, 2013's

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:18.360
The Lone Ranger deserves a prominent place.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.330
The film's struggle began years before release
as producers searched for ways to wrangle

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.770
the budget down from a proposed $260 million.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:30.490
When cameras finally rolled, the budget quickly
spiraled out of control again, to the point

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.490
where production was briefly shut down over
cost concerns.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:37.819
The film's release date was also delayed several
times, and in the meantime, images of star

00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:42.819
Johnny Depp in full regalia as the Ranger's
sidekick Tonto were deemed both ridiculous

00:07:42.819 --> 00:07:43.889
and insulting.

00:07:43.889 --> 00:07:48.409
With Armie Hammer in the title role, the film
opened to wildly indifferent audiences and

00:07:48.409 --> 00:07:50.550
brutal mockery from critics.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:54.610
"The men you seek think you are dead Kemosabe."

00:07:54.610 --> 00:08:00.749
All in all, Disney and The Lone Ranger got
bucked off to the tune of a $190 million loss.

00:08:00.749 --> 00:08:01.969
John Carter

00:08:01.969 --> 00:08:04.830
There are sickeningly pricey flops, and then
there's John Carter.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.330
"Who is that?"

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.669
The movie was a decades-long effort to adapt
author Edgar Rice Burroughs' Barsoom novels

00:08:10.669 --> 00:08:13.219
into an epic, live-action Disney film.

00:08:13.219 --> 00:08:17.819
When John Carter finally made it to screens
in 2012, the sci-fi action flick just wasn't

00:08:17.819 --> 00:08:19.139
worth the long wait.

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:20.650
Just how bad was it?

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.930
"Go on, shock me."

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:28.339
Star Taylor Kitsch led a production that found
a way to be stupendously dull, despite the

00:08:28.339 --> 00:08:32.460
fact the movie ballooned up a $250 million
budget.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.440
Audiences were confused, critics were bored,
and the film's massive marketing push left

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.440
it deeper in the red than almost any film
to date.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.591
Its worldwide take of $284 million didn't
come close to covering costs, and for the

00:08:44.591 --> 00:08:48.940
second year in a row, Disney was responsible
for one of the biggest flops of all time,

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:52.029
a breathtaking $200 million loss.

00:08:52.029 --> 00:08:55.009
Disney's dreams of a John Carter franchise?

00:08:55.009 --> 00:09:00.240
"Let them be crushed like unhatched eggs!"

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.339
Thanks for watching!

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:05.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.790
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

